<title>FINLAND: Finland mulls converting benchmarks to euro in '99.</title>
<headline>Finland mulls converting benchmarks to euro in '99.</headline>
<dateline>HELSINKI 1997-06-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Finnish finance ministry is considering converting its markka-denominated benchmark bonds to euro-denomination at the planned launch of economic and monetary union (EMU) in 1999, a senior ministry official said on Thursday.</p>
<p>Speaking at a seminar arranged by training institute IIR Finland, acting finance director Inga-Maria Grohn said a decision whether to do so could be expected after summer.</p>
<p>&quot;Government benchmark bonds will be converted to euro (at the latest) in 2002, but we are considering a conversion in 1999&quot;, Grohn said. @</p>
<p>She also said the ministry was considering to expand its market maker group of 10 banks with some from the core EMU area.</p>
<p>She expected the Finnish interest rate premium over core Europe to continue to fall as EMU draws nearer.</p>
<p>--Helsinki newsroom +358-9-680 50 245, news@reuters.fi</p>
